This generates endpoint methods for all PHP client classes. The general flow of the endpoint implementation is shown below:

```php
/**
 * @param ?array{baseUrl?: string} $options
 * @returns GetUserResponse
 */
public function get(?array $options): GetUserResponse
{
    try {
        $response = $this->client->sendRequest(...);
        $statusCode = $response->getStatusCode();
        if ($statusCode >= 200 && $statusCode < 400) {
            return GetUserResponse::fromJson($response->getBody->getContents());
        }
    } catch (JsonException $e) {
        throw new Exception("Failed to deserialize response", 0, $e);
    } catch (ClientExceptionInterface $e) {
        throw new Exception($e->getMessage());
    }
    throw new Exception("Error with status code " . $statusCode);
}
```

The implementation here is incomplete, but the general structure of each endpoint is implemented by the `EndpointGenerator` (which is largely inspired by the C#-equivalent).

Future changes will actually generate the in-lined request type, deserialize the JSON response, and get everything working.

